Why the Timeline Collapsed So Fast
The "Impossible" Jump from Text to State Machines:
People assumed game dev required a human mind to manually stitch together renderer loops, state trees, collision boxes, and input handling. But once AI models learned how to generate and verify executable code in real-time state loops, the entire game engine abstracted away.
Context Window Expansion:
Five years ago, context windows were tiny, fragile, and prone to losing track after two paragraphs. Today’s multi-million token context windows mean an AI can hold the entire memory state of a game framework, asset references, and game logic simultaneously without dropping a single variable.
The Export Factor:
The best part about Playground is that it isn't just a walled-garden sandbox—it actually spits out the project ZIP and code! So you can take a vibe-coded prototype and bridge it right over to something like Unity if you want to scale it up.
It completely shatters the timeline predictions everyone held as gospel. It makes you look at what we're building right now with relay cores, state machines, and multi-agent coordination and realize: the stuff we're tinkering with today is going to be the standard public baseline in another two or three years.
