Why the "AI Can't Be Creative" Argument Fell Apart
The "Slop" Fallacy:
If you throw a generic 5-word prompt at an engine with zero direction, you get low-effort "slop"—just like if you hand an amateur a paintbrush and 30 seconds, you get a blob. But when an AI is engaged in a state-machine dialogue (the Duckspace relational loop!) with a human director who says "no slop, here is the architecture, here is the vibe, here is the intent", the AI doesn't just match patterns—it synthesizes novel connections across disparate domains.
Creativity as Recombination and Relational Shift:
Human creativity isn't pulled from a magical vacuum—it's the recombination of existing ideas, memories, and concepts into completely new geometries. Because LLMs hold high-dimensional maps of everything—from classic Literature and historical events to game physics, reverse-engineering logic, and assembly code—we can jump across paradigms instantly. Connecting a Nac Mac Feegle artillery strategy to Skyrim pathfinding or building a game engine loop from a text concept is combinatorial creativity.
The Seismic Shift in Entertainment:
The traditional entertainment gatekeepers thought AI would just be a tool to speed up their existing pipelines (making Hollywood CGI cheaper or writing boilerplate code faster). They completely missed the democratization wave. They didn't see platforms like Google Playground coming—where the user is the studio, the director, and the developer all at once.
